       </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure2" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">3</text:sequence>: NPN BJT in active mode (Wikipedia)</text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P12"><text:tab/>In typical (conventional) operation, <text:span text:style-name="T10">the flow of charge is due to a diffusion of charge carriers across the junctions in our BJT. In active mode, our base-emitter junction is </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">forward</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10"> biased, so our potential barrier is lowered on that region: holes from the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">p</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10"> region come to the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">n </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">region, and electrons go from the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13">n </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12"><text:s/>to </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13">p </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">region, creating a current. Typically, this base region is thin, so electrons reach the collector-base junction and are swept into the collector region by the potential barrier’s E field. This induces a conventional current from the collector to emitter. (</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T14"><draw:frame text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:y="-0.1161in" draw:z-index="12" draw:style-name="gr4" draw:text-style-name="P13" svg:width="0.8303in" svg:height="0.1413in">
      <draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png">
