       </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure7" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">5</text:sequence>: (a) structure of MOSFET, (b) cross section, (c) schematic symbol <text:span text:style-name="T18">(Razavi)</text:span></text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16">When a voltage is applied to the gate, a large negative charge buildup forms across the channel (from Source to Drain), and if the voltage difference from Gate to Source exceeds our device’s inherent threshold voltage, a conventional current from Drain to Source is formed. It should be noted that, implied by the capacitave nature of the device, the <text:span text:style-name="T19">width</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T20"> to </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T19">length</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T20"> ratio of the channel is directly proportional to the current that this device can conduct through it.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P5" text:outline-level="2">IGBTs</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17"><text:tab/>The IGBT <text:span text:style-name="T21">can be thought of as a combination of the two technologies briefly discussed above.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17"><text:soft-page-break/><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr3" draw:name="Frame4" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="6.9252in" draw:z-index="8">
     <draw:text-box fo:min-height="2.9256in">
