        </draw:image>
       </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure4" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">7</text:sequence>: IGBT Equivalent Circuit</text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T22">From a high level, our cross section (figure </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T22"><text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="value" text:ref-name="refFigure3">6</text:sequence-ref></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T22">) is laid similarly to a MOSFET, noting the isolated gate. When we correctly apply a </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T23">gate-source voltage</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T22">, a channel forms across the p region (our body region), and “connects” our n+ source region to our drain. However, our drain is not one region, its multiple: a drain drift region (n-), a buffer (n+), and an injecting layer (p+). </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T23">So, within the drain region, we have formed a pn junction. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T23">This buffer layer is not explicitly necessary. The presence of it indicates that this is a punch-through IGBT. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T24">Including this region means that we have a thinner potential barrier, which drops our on-</text:span><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T24">state losses. However, this means our reverse-blocking ability is diminished, and our device will only work in forward biased mode (Vds &gt; 0). Removing this layer creates a symmetric (non punch-through) IGBT, and can block both forward and reverse voltages.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T24">Once the gate-source voltage passes the threshold, our channel forms beneath the gate, allowing current to flow. Our n- drain drift region is essentially shorted to the n+ source region (exactly like a MOSFET), and </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T25">Considering the physical construction, we can see that the IGBT can be equivalently modeled as a Darlington pair, with a MOSFET as the driver and a pnp transistor as our main device. Note that our</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20"><office:annotation office:name="__Annotation__408_2023087085" loext:resolved="false">
     <dc:creator>james ryan</dc:creator>
     <dc:date>2026-03-02T10:12:48.829838986</dc:date>
     <meta:creator-initials>jr</meta:creator-initials>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment">Ld</text:p>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment"/>
    </office:annotation>The structure in figure <text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="value" text:ref-name="refFigure3">6</text:sequence-ref><text:s/>is quite similar to the structure of a MOSFET, <text:span text:style-name="T22">s</text:span>pecifically, <text:span text:style-name="T22">when we apply a voltage to the gate, we form a channel from the source n+ region to the “drain drift” n- region.</text:span> But, compared to a FET, there is a key difference: the p+ layer which forms our drain creates a pn junction which allows us to inject minority carriers into the drain of what would be our MOSFET. <text:span text:style-name="T26">The construction of an IGBT can vary a bit: it can either be a punch-through or non-punch-through structure. A punch-through structure has a drift region that is about half the thickness of a non-punch-through structure, which has the benefit of incurring lower losses in the on-state, but due to the buffer layer, we greatly diminish the reverse-blocking capability of our structure.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T26">I</text:span>n the equilibrium (or, “off”) state, there is no voltage applied on the gate, so neither channel forms, <text:span text:style-name="T26">and a small leakage current is present. This is close to how our MOSFET operates at equilibrium.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T26">In the “on” state, our gate-source voltage is large enough to sufficiently form the channel, inducing a current flow, shorting our drift region and our n+ source region. As a result of this, holes in our p+ drain region are induced to flow into our drift region </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T25">via charge carrier drift and diffusion, and recombine in the </text:span><office:annotation office:name="__Annotation__256_2126872237" loext:resolved="false">
     <dc:creator>james ryan</dc:creator>
     <dc:date>2026-03-01T03:02:37.170901588</dc:date>
     <meta:creator-initials>jr</meta:creator-initials>
     <text:p text:style-name="Comment">Go back and format all these nicely<text:line-break/></text:p>
    </office:annotation><text:span text:style-name="T25">p</text:span><office:annotation-end office:name="__Annotation__256_2126872237"/><text:span text:style-name="T25"> body region. Essentially, the junction between the body region and the drift region collects the attracted holes, and the device operates like a pnp BJT, if it had a disproportionately thick collector region. </text:span><office:annotation-end office:name="__Annotation__408_2023087085"/></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P22" text:outline-level="1"><text:soft-page-break/>Benefits</text:h>
   <text:h text:style-name="P23" text:outline-level="1">Example: Usage in an Induction Heating Circuit</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P24"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr3" draw:name="Frame3" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="6.9252in" draw:z-index="4">
     <draw:text-box fo:min-height="4.1799in">
