          AAAAwJ7gAAAAAPYEBwAAALAnOAAAAIA9wQEAAADsCQ4AAKDsFr0AAAA0SURBVABgT3AAAAAA
          e4IDAAAA2BMcAAAAwJ7gAAAAAPYEBwAAALAnOAAAAIA9wQEAAADsBbhrZye6Gj2LAAAAAElF
          TkSuQmCC
         </office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
       </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure5" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">8</text:sequence>: IGBT-based induction heater circuit (hackatronic.com)</text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T9">In an induction heater, our main goal is to induce heat into another piece of metal using some kind of coil. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T27">Conceptually, the goal is to harness an AC power source to induce a magnetic field around our coil. This alternating magnetic field then generates current in metal which is in proximity to it, which in turn induces a circulating current in that metal. These currents are called eddy currents. Metal is imperfect, so this current encounters resistance as it circulates, and as we learned in circuit analysis, this generates energy, which in our case becomes thermal heat. So, our main goal is to create a circuit which can generate a lot of circulating currents, in order to produce a lot of heat in our neighboring metal very quickly</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P26"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T27">We have a sample circuit shown in figure </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T27"><text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="value" text:ref-name="refFigure5">8</text:sequence-ref></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T27">, an IGBT-based induction heater. Our main coil (L1 and L2) is connected to a driver circuit. This driver controls the flow of our current from the 12-volt power supply, where our IGBTs (Q1 and Q2, SKU HGTG40N60B3) act as an automatic switch. We have two large capacitors (C1 and C2) for two reasons: adding capacitors will offset our large power factor, which is a result of having a large inductance in our circuit, and we can take advantage of this to produce a resonant LC tank circuit, to produce an oscillating current in our circuit. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T28">Our IGBTs are set up in a multivibrator configuration: as our capacitors charge to a threshold, our center-tapped transformer has current going down it in one direction. When we reach the threshold, the polarity across the coil reverses, and our current also reverses. This change in current induces the magnetic </text:span><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T28">field. We specifically use IGBTs in this configuration since they are well-suited for the large alternating currents we are experiencing in the control circuitry. </text:span></text:p>
  </office:text>
 </office:body>
</office:document>